  "title": "Equations of Lines and Planes",
  "body": " Equations of Lines and Planes   The line in xy-plane is determined by a point and its direction vector . Let be a vector parallel to the line: , and be an arbitrary point on the line. Let and be the position vectors of and , respectively, as shown in the figure below.    From the figure, we can see that the vector is parallel to the direction vector . Therefore, there exists a scalar such that: . This gives the vector equation of the line: . Each value of parameter gives the position vector of a point on the line, . This parametric representation allows us to describe all points on the line using a single parameter . Positive values of correspond to points on the line that lie on one side of , whereas negative values of correspond to points that lie on the other side of .  If , the parametric equations of the line can be written in component form as: . or, . The symmetric form of the line equation can be written as: .    Find the parametric and symmetric equations of the line passing through the point and parallel to the vector .    The parametric equations of the line are: . The symmetric form of the line equation is: .      Find the parametric and symmetric equations of the line passing through the point and point .    The direction vector of the line is: . The parametric equations of the line are: . The symmetric form of the line equation is: .      Skew Lines  Two lines in space are said to be skew if they are not parallel and do not intersect. Skew lines exist in three-dimensional space and can be described using parametric equations. For example, the lines and are skew if their direction vectors and are not parallel and the lines do not intersect.      Planes   A plane in space is a flat, two-dimensional surface that extends infinitely in all directions. A plane is defined by a point in the plane and a normal vector to the plane . Let be any point in the plane, and let and be the position vectors of and , respectively. Then the vector is perpendicular to , so their dot product is zero. Hence the vector equation of the plane is: .    If , , and , then . This is known as the scalar equation of the plane. The general form of the equation of the plane is , where . For example, the plane has a normal vector .    Find the equation of the plane that passes through the point and has a normal vector . Find the intercepts of the plane.    The equation of the plane is , or equivalently, . The intercepts are , , and . To find the intercepts, set two variables to zero and solve for the remaining variable.      Find the equation of the plane that passes through the point , , and .    The vectors and are parallel, so the three points are collinear. Therefore, no unique plane passes through all three points.      Find the equation of the plane that passes through the point , , and .    The vectors and are not parallel, so the three points are not collinear. A normal vector to the plane is . Therefore, the equation of the plane is , or equivalently, .      Find the point at which the line with parametric equations , , and intersects the plane .    Substituting the parametric equations into the plane equation gives , which simplifies to . Therefore, , and the point of intersection is , , and , or .      Find the angle between the planes and . Find symmetric equations for the line of intersection of these two planes.    The normal vectors to the planes are and . Therefore, the angle between the planes is   A direction vector for the line of intersection is . Setting and solving the plane equations gives the point on the line. Therefore, symmetric equations for the line are .       In three-dimensional space, a single linear equation in , , and defines a plane. Because two non-parallel planes intersect along a shared path, a system of two linear equations can be used to describe a line. Any coordinate point that satisfy both and lie on both of these planes at once, and so the pair of linear equations represents the line of intersection of the planes (if they are not parallel).     Distance from a Point to a Plane    A formula for measuring the distance from a point to the plane .  Let be the distance from to the plane. Consider any arbitrary point on the plane, so that direction vector . The scalar projection of onto the normal vector is      Find the distance from the point to the plane .    Using the distance formula, Therefore, the distance is .      Find the distance from the plane to the plane .    To calculate the distance between the planes, first note that their normal vectors are parallel. Therefore, the planes are parallel. Choose a point on the first plane, such as . Then calculate the distance from this point to the second plane, using the distance formula, Therefore, the distance is .      Find the distance between the skew lines and .    The direction vectors of the lines are and . A vector perpendicular to both lines is The distance between the lines is the distance from to the plane through with normal vector . Using the distance formula, Therefore, the distance is .      Find the distance between the skew lines and .    The direction vectors of the lines are and . A vector perpendicular to both lines is The distance between the lines is the distance from to the plane through with normal vector . Using the distance formula, Therefore, the distance is .        Find the distance between the skew lines and .    Find the distance between a point to the line .    Find the distance between the point and the plane .    Find the equation of the line through the point and parallel to the vector .    Find the parametric equation of the line through the point  and parallel to the vector .    and parallel to the vector .     and parallel to the line .       Find parametric equations and symmetric equations for the line.   through the origin and the point .    through the points and perpendicular to both and .    through the point and parallel to the line .       Find parametric equations for the line of intersection of the planes and .    Is the line through the points and parallel to the line through the points and ?    Is the line through the points and perpendicular to the line through the points and ?    Find parametric equations for the line segment from to .    Find an equation of the plane through the points , , and .    Find the point of intersection of the line and the plane .    Where does the line through the points and intersect the plane ?    Find the cosine of the angle between the planes and .    Determine whether the planes are parallel, perpendicular, or neither.    and      and      and        Find an equation for the plane consisting of all points that are equidistant from the points and .    Show that the distance between the parallel planes and is .   Find a point on the first plane and use the distance formula to find its distance from the second plane. For example, you can choose a point on the first plane such that . Then use the distance formula to find the distance from this point to the second plane.     "
