  "body": " Fields   A field describes a physical quantity or mathematical function that exists throughout space. Depending on what is assigned to each point, fields fall into two main categories:   Scalar field: Assigns a single number (a scalar) to each point in space. Examples include temperature in a room, atmospheric pressure, or gravitational potential energy.  Vector field: Assigns both a magnitude and a direction (a vector) to each point in space. Examples include a gravitational force field, an electric field, or fluid velocity vectors across a stream.   The color and contour map above illustrates a 2D scalar field: each grid point has a single numerical value (elevation) shown by color and contour spacing, with tighter contours indicating steeper slopes.    The Line Integrals  A line integral (also called a path integral) sums up a field along a curve parameterized by for . Instead of slicing an interval into segments , you chop the curve into tiny pieces of arc length or displacement .   Scalar Line Integral: Evaluates a scalar function along a curve and multiplies each piece by its arc length : Physical meaning: If represents linear mass density along a curved wire, gives the total mass of the wire. If , the integral equals the arc length of .    Vector Line Integral: Measures how much of a vector field points along the direction of motion along curve using the dot product : Physical meaning: When is a force field, equals the work done moving a particle along . If is perpendicular to the path everywhere, the dot product is zero and no work is done along that curve.   Beyond these two types, there are some other forms of line integrals also appear in multivariable calculus.     Scalar Line Integral ( ) Evaluate , where is the line segment from to .       Parameterize the curve : A straight line from to for is: so and .    Compute the speed \/ arc-length factor :  Thus .    Substitute into the integral and evaluate:            Vector Line Integral ( ) Evaluate the work for the vector field along the quarter-circle of radius from to in the first quadrant.       Parameterize the curve : A circle of radius traversed counterclockwise from angle to is: so and .    Differentiate and evaluate :      Take the dot product and integrate:     Notice that the dot product is negative throughout the path because circulates clockwise while the quarter-circle moves counterclockwise.  At (start of the path): . The arrow points straight down.At (end of the path): . The arrow points straight right. At (midpoint): The field points down and right tangent to a clockwise circle.  At , moving along the quarter-circle takes you upward ( ).At , moving along the quarter-circle takes you leftward ( )         For a curve parameterized by on , what is the arc-length differential in terms of ?   Differentiate each component of , square both derivatives, sum them, and take the square root.     Evaluate where is the straight line segment from to .   Integrating a constant with respect to equals times the geometric length of the curve.     Let be the line segment from to , parameterized by for . What is ?  Hint: Express in terms of , find , and integrate from to .    Suppose along curve from to . What is along the same curve traversed in reverse from to ?  Hint: Does the physical length of a tiny piece of wire change when you count from the other end?    A force field acts on a particle moving along from to . What is the integrand ?  Hint: Substitute and into , find , and compute the dot product.    Using the same field and path for from the previous question, what is the total work ?  Hint: Integrate term by term from to .    Let and let be the unit circle traversed once counterclockwise from back to . What is the circulation ?    A vector field is conservative with potential function . What is along any smooth path from to ?      "
